<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Klaipėda Revolt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1923</span> · Also known as Memel Revolt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Klaipėda</a> Revolt took place in January 1923, when Lithuanian irregulars, organised secretly by the Lithuanian government and presented as a local uprising, seized the Klaipėda Region from its French administration. The region had been detached from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> and placed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>. The Allies accepted the result, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda-convention">Klaipėda Convention</a> of 1924 made the region an autonomous part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/klaipeda-revolt.jpg" alt="Klaipėda Revolt" width="320" height="296" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: original SVG file created by Matthead (based on East_Prussia_1939.kpg from English Wikipedia) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:East_Prussia_1923-1939.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
